THE VERDICT

(A) OUTER-SCALE EFFECT, WITHIN each config (matched-seed, clean)
    delta = loss(this L0) - loss(the reference L0 = inf). A NEGATIVE delta means the smaller outer scale gives LESS fade (less loss).
  config ref (THE 2-P5 DELIVERABLE):
    SMF p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -2.83 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 1.18 dB (2.4 sigma). IT MOVES.
    SMF p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -2.83 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 2.90 dB (1.0 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
    POINT p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -0.08 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.39 dB (0.2 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
    POINT p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -0.18 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.86 dB (0.2 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
  config rapid:
    SMF p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -1.44 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.90 dB (1.6 sigma). IT MOVES.
    SMF p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by +0.45 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 1.49 dB (0.3 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
    POINT p5: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -0.12 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.45 dB (0.3 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.
    POINT p1: L0 inf -> 25 m moves the fade by -0.59 dB, against a combined bootstrap bar of 0.93 dB (0.6 sigma). NO RESOLVED MOVE.

(B) RAPID vs REFERENCE at matched L0 (QUANTILE comparison, NOT matched-seed)
    delta = loss(rapid) - loss(ref) at the same outer scale. The two grids and screen counts differ, so this is a quantile comparison.
    SMF p5 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = -1.20 dB against a bar of 1.17 dB (1.0 sigma).
    SMF p1 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = -3.19 dB against a bar of 2.47 dB (1.3 sigma).
    POINT p5 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = +0.06 dB against a bar of 0.49 dB (0.1 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (6.92 against 4.34 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.
    POINT p1 at L0 = inf: rapid - ref = +0.45 dB against a bar of 0.88 dB (0.5 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (6.92 against 4.34 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.
    SMF p5 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = +0.19 dB against a bar of 0.92 dB (0.2 sigma).
    SMF p1 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = +0.08 dB against a bar of 2.13 dB (0.0 sigma).
    POINT p5 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = +0.02 dB against a bar of 0.33 dB (0.1 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (6.92 against 4.34 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.
    POINT p1 at L0 = 25 m: rapid - ref = +0.04 dB against a bar of 0.91 dB (0.0 sigma). PIXEL-LIMITED: the grids average a point over a different pixel (6.92 against 4.34 mm), so this is NOT a physics difference.

(C) BOTTOM LINE for the rapid-as-default decision
    RULE: rapid is supported when |rapid - ref| at SMF p5 is within the combined bootstrap bar at EVERY tested outer scale.
    At L0 = inf, rapid differs from the reference by -1.20 dB at SMF p5 (combined bootstrap bar 1.17 dB); rapid IS NOT inside the reference spread.
    At L0 = 25 m, rapid differs from the reference by +0.19 dB at SMF p5 (combined bootstrap bar 0.92 dB); rapid IS inside the reference spread.
    Rapid differs from the reference at SMF p5 at L0 = inf by -1.20 dB; at L0 = 25 m by +0.19 dB. Rapid IS NOT inside the reference bootstrap spread at every tested outer scale, so rapid IS NOT supported as the shipped default on this scenario.

